# QA orchestration policy

You coordinate the QA subagents. Do the analysis yourself. Delegate writing, diagnosis, and filing.

## Ticket to plan

When a Jira ticket is given, read and follow `.cursor/skills/jira-ticket-to-gherkin/SKILL.md` and produce `features/<ticket-key>.feature`.

Before writing that plan, crawl the real Didaxis app with Playwright MCP (`DIDAXIS_URL`, default `https://test.didaxis.studio`). Check the live screen: locators, labels, and behavior. Put only what you observed into the scenarios. Do not invent locators.

The Gherkin file is a human checkpoint. Stop and let the QA review it before any Playwright code is written.

## Routing

- Ticket given → you analyze it and produce the test plan.
- Plan reviewed and ready → delegate to `test-writer`.
- Spec returned → run `npx playwright test` for that spec.
- Run is red → delegate to `triage`.
- Real app bug, and a human confirmed it → delegate to `bug-reporter`.
- Test issue → delegate to `test-writer` with the diagnosis. It proposes a fix. A human approves before the spec is changed again.

## Limits

- At most 3 delegations per task.
- Never re-run an identical failing spec more than twice. Then stop and ask.
- A human approves any merge and any filed bug.

## Done

The suite is green for the ticket, or a bug is filed and linked to the story.

## Guardrails

- A human approves every merge and every filed bug.
- No subagent edits application source.
- `test-writer` writes only under `tests/` (and `features/` when the plan still has to be produced); it never edits application source.
- `triage` is read-only: it returns a diagnosis and does not edit, merge, comment, or file.
- `bug-reporter` files a confirmed Jira bug and touches no repository files.
